v class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A stable matter-bearing background in the same action</h2><p>The completed flat-family obstruction led to a different exact branch: U=-ib, E=0 and equal positive lowest-mode matter, with every diamond holonomy -1. The scalar orbit is nonlinearly stable for 0≤κ&lt;Nρ(10-√10)/(120(6+√2)), where ρ=√(8+2√10) and each species norm is N. The bound is sufficient and conservative.</p><p>A separate compactness argument establishes a stable global minimizing set at every finite rank. It proves neither individual-orbit stability at higher rank nor attraction. The old flat-family instability is preserved. 40 exact constructor checks and two finite nonlinear numerical campaigns passed; no experimental data. Independent review pending, physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#curved-ground-v01">Read the curved-orbit stability theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="resonance-closure-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The six open resonances now have nonlinear witnesses</h2><p>Exact fixed-point sectors of the full nonlinear flow exclude the resonant modes while retaining a nonzero averaged matter drift. S3 closes t=2/3, 8/3 and 16/(4±√2); C4 closes 16/(6±√2). No detuning or action change is used.</p><p>The nonzero neutral flat family is therefore nonlinearly orbitally unstable across a&gt;0, κ≥0 at finite rank, conditional on the declared action and the preceding theorem. Positive-stiffness empty vacuum stability is preserved; other backgrounds remain unclassified. 104 exact checks and six numerical campaigns; independent review pending; physical promotion 0. Earlier dated entries below preserve their historical boundaries.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#resonance-closure-v01">Read the six-case closure theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="nonlinear-orbital-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A nonlinear departure theorem with six open resonances</h2><p>Within the unchanged candidate action, fixed-norm Gauss-admissible perturbations can approach the nonzero flat stationary orbit while exact trajectories reach fixed higher-simplex matter stock on O(1/ε) time. The proof uses conserved co-moving matter coherence, exact Coulomb dynamics and a finite-Fourier averaging estimate; four field resonances have separate force-canceling constructions.</p><p>Six positive values of t=aκ/r remain open: 2/3, 8/3, 16/(6±√2), and 16/(4±√2). Zero stiffness instead has an exact holonomy drift counterexample. The positive-stiffness empty vacuum remains stable. 42 exact constructor checks and three numerical campaigns passed; no experimental data or physical promotion.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#nonlinear-orbital-v01">Read the theorem and its parameter boundary →</a></article><article class="claim" id="gauge-reduced-modes-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Imaginary frequencies can retain secular growth</h2><p>The exact Gauss reduction leaves 14 link oscillator pairs, 28 algebraic matter pairs and one neutral zero pair. Five positive link stiffnesses are 6, 6±√2 and 4±√2. The nonzero neutral flat family has persistent nongauge Jordan chains, including at isolated forcing cancellations and field resonances. Positive magnetic stiffness alone does not make that family linearly bounded.</p><p>The empty vacuum gauge orbit is separately proved nonlinearly Lyapunov stable for positive stiffness; zero stiffness admits exact drift instability. Nonlinear stability of the nonzero family remains open. 50 exact constructor checks passed; no experimental data were used. Physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#gauge-reduced-modes-v01">Read the reduction and stability theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="joint-hamiltonian-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Neutral matter can drive initially flat links</h2><p>The declared joint Hamiltonian has first-class Gauss constraints, conserved energy and matter norms, reciprocal backreaction and global temporal-gauge solutions. A single canonical fundamental species fails closed full-U(r) neutrality; an explicit opposite-charge dynamical partner repairs that branch. A neutral two-node scalar preparation gives diamond phase 2as² + O(s³) and curvature defect 2a²s⁴ + O(s⁵).</p><p>28 exact constructor checks and a separately typed numerical replay passed. This is conditional dynamics under an added action and species, with independent review pending and physical promotion 0. Next gate: admissible neutral stationary backgrounds and gauge-reduced modes.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#joint-hamiltonian-v01">Read the theorem and replay →</a></article><article class="claim" id="history-comparison-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October2026 · Reading edition0.14</div><h2>Different histories require more than matching frequencies</h2><p>The star29 and barycentric149 carriers agree on the old code. An unrestricted full-generator star embedding exists, but S4-equivariance fails because its invariant √5 pair is absent from the target. A163-direction retained-record completion avoids losing old detail. Exact inherited skew dynamics cannot populate initially blank details; native excitation remains OPEN.</p><a class="text-link" href="/history-comparison/">Inspect history and source gates →</a></article><article class="claim" id="refinement-tower-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition0.13</div><h2>Refinement details need inherited calibration</h2><p>The two-level15→149→2745 tower has an exact edge-detail mismatch2 versus4 under sequential versus fresh final calibration. Transporting each level detail pairing restores consistency for regrouping the same sequence. Coarse observations compose,17 complete effects retain both detail outcomes, and full carrier growth is explicit. Native history and physical adoption remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/refinement-tower/">Inspect refinement coherence →</a></article><article class="claim" id="metric-selection-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition0.12</div><h2>Metric and observable selection has explicit premises</h2><p>Native fixed-carrier fidelity selectsI and coordinate stocks. Across ordinary subdivision, exact metric and observer witnesses prove remaining freedom, and arbitrary diagonal fine metrics fail full generator inheritance. Affine readout, calibrated orthogonal residual and blind observation uniquely select a conditional extension; adoption remains open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/metric-selection/">Inspect metric and observer selection →</a></article><article class="claim" id="subdivision-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.11</div><h2>Subdivision has three separate compatibility tests</h2><p>The actual source map has factorial norm factors1,2,6,24. Scalar and naive metric normalization fail the generator gate. A conditional retraction/complement metric satisfies norm, boundary and generator equations, while retaining134 fine directions and leaving native metric/local readout selection open. The original Euclidean no-go remains intact.</p><a class="text-link" href="/subdivision/">Inspect subdivision compatibility →</a></article><article class="claim" id="metric-admission-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.10</div><h2>Source-defined metrics receive separate clock tests</h2><p>The invariant metric family keeps three positive bands. The geometric Gram family can have five but fails the default clock through an exact ratio identity. Degree-weighted glued carriers fail a separate commensurability test. The adjoint/mass metric conventions and all exclusions remain explicit; native selection is open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/metric-admission/">Inspect source metric admission →</a></article><article class="claim" id="native-admission-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.9</div><h2>Native spectral admission has explicit residual floors</h2><p>The default clock has exact common-rate residual minima3,7√15−25 and2 across the three declared unit-metric classes. Arbitrary identical copies cannot create exact full-generator agreement. A retained endpoint-alias witness has zero endpoint error despite a nonzero generator residual, keeping the error-budget inference scoped.</p><a class="text-link" href="/native-admission/">Inspect native residuals and aliases →</a></article><article class="claim" id="programmed-clock-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.8</div><h2>A supplied clock, an engineered Hodge path, and two scoped limits</h2><p>The finite carrier has a constant history Hamiltonian. Rational path metrics and identity padding give an ordinary graph-Hodge representation, with clock and complete return accounted. Odd full-carrier equivalence and unit-metric single-tetrahedron full embedding have explicit obstructions. Native operator/source admission and protected export remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/programmed-clock/">Inspect the coherent clock and admission gate →</a></article><article class="claim" id="joint-carrier-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.7</div><h2>A finite-window joint carrier with complete accounting</h2><p>A supplied reversible gate retains each old state and receipt. A finite master clock schedules B payload resets using B prepared donors; all consumed blocks become outgoing records. The construction proves a declared window, shows dirty-reuse failure after wrap, and keeps native coupling, physical resources and complete restart open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/joint-carrier/">Inspect clock, memory and reset supply →</a></article><article class="claim" id="hodge-rsp-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.6</div><h2>The strict Hodge realization class has an explicit obstruction</h2><p>An exact total finite-image factor of reversible Hodge evolution can decode only RSP fixed states. Prepared trace encoding remains possible before wrap. The audit separates finite-image, finite-order, stable-recurrence and conserved-stock assumptions; broader native realization and human review remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/realization/">Inspect the realization contracts →</a></article><article class="claim" id="rsp1-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.5</div><h2>A complete finite candidate, with its stopping debts visible</h2><p>RSP-1 drives two source-bound close/hold response choices from prepared stock and finite bank registers. Exact conditional termination, close pulse balance and hold latching are recorded. Parallel constructor checks and a separate AI scope audit passed; native realization and independent human review remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/source-programme/">Compare the paired programmes →</a></article><article class="claim" id="response-contract-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.4</div><h2>The missing response law is now a typed source contract</h2><p>The finite response fiber has 2^i masks on i intact-null addresses. Count receipts and address receipts have different information requirements. A structural validator and synthetic specimen make the source gate reviewable. The prior presentation review is acknowledged; this new development result awaits independent adversarial review.</p><a class="text-link" href="/source-contract/">Explore the contract →</a></article><article class="claim" id="dependency-update-v03"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.3</div><h2>Polarity and action dependencies</h2><p>Source-bound premises, constitutive choices and evaluation gates now have an explicit scoped graph. Eight change scenarios identify affected uses while retaining conditional mathematics. Absolute clock and native event-source gates remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/dependencies/">Explore the dependencies →</a></article><article class="update-card"><span class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · theorem milestone v0.1</span><h2>A typed gauge-transport construction and its curvature boundary</h2><p>The dedicated theorem manuscript proves local frame covariance, signed tetrahedral relabeling covariance and conserved support for unitary Hasse-link transport. On the filled tetrahedron, a nilpotent transport boundary is pure gauge. Curved transport remains admissible with an explicit correction to the Hodge-square identity.</p><p>A single incidence phase of π/2 gives the exact positive frequency set √2, 2, √6 with multiplicities 1, 5, 1. The result supplies a candidate finite analogue test; it does not derive Standard Model couplings. Independent review and physical correspondence remain pending; physical promotion 0.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#gauge-transport-v01">Read theorem and replay →</a> · <a class="text-link" href="/sources/gauge-transport-v0.1/GAUGE_BRIDGE_CONTRACT_v0.1.json">Bridge contract ↓</a></p></article><article class="update-card"><span class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · source intake v0.1</span><h2>Additional-workstream narrative manuscript captured</h2><p>Review candidate 0.15 is preserved as a separate synthesis with its original PDF hash, complete text extract and page index. Intake preserves source status and leaves scientific replay, independent review and physical correspondence gates unchanged.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#narrative-v015">Read the manuscript →</a> · <a class="text-link" href="/sources/narrative-v0.15/INGEST_RECEIPT_v0.1.json">Intake record ↓</a></p></article><article><time>13 October 2026</time><h3>Receipt gap and common deadline</h3><p>34 write slots; 44 versus 66 total-slot comparisons. Dimensionless probability does not select physical energy or area.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R40">R40 ↗</a></article><article><time>13 October 2026</time><h3>Native signature and refinement</h3><p>Fixed-carrier skew flow does not choose a spatial refinement. Strict unit-readout/intertwining interface fails.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R29">R29 ↗</a> <a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R30">R30 ↗</a> <a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R34">R34 ↗</a></article><article><time>13 October 2026</time><h3>POC-1 custody gap</h3><p>Original sidecar bytes and eligible target-hidden native episode remain required before a score.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R35">R35 ↗</a></article><article><time>9–11 October 2026</time><h3>Predictive masks and typed event inputs</h3><p>Structural predicates, current observations and event source laws retain separate roles.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R09">R09 ↗</a> <a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R28">R28 ↗</a></article></details></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>Historical planning snapshot.</h2><details class="atlas-collection"><summary>Inspect the earlier work plan <span>Historical planning, not current status</span></summary></div></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>The next scientific priority.</h2><p><strong>Source the native event-response law and independently review the corrected conditional domain.</strong> The presentation now exposes every ledger row and its reuse limits. The remaining hinge is scientific: an independently sourced constitutive response, complete proof dependencies and an eligible test. A better reading surface does not supply that law.</p><p>Priority judgment: <strong>96/100</strong>. This score ranks expected progress and information value; it is not a measured closure percentage.</p></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">04</div><div><h2>Coverage limitations.</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>One miss